  "strategy": "Approach and Rationale\nWe introduce a novel optimization framework combining sparse convolutional operators with non-convex structured regularization to minimize the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap integral. Our approach leverages the following key innovations:\n1. Sparse filters: We use overcomplete expansion in filter dictionaries derived from periodic sequences\n2. Multi-scale analysis: Three optimized resolution levels (400, 600, 800 points) with progressive refinement\n3. Structured sparsity: Group LASSO on shift-invariant features via frequency domain regularization\n4. Projected gradient with Anderson acceleration: Fast convergence guaranteed by inertial methods\n\nThis addresses the high-dimensional optimization bottleneck by combining dimensionality reduction (sparse filters) with structured regularization (feature group sparsity) and aggressive dimensionality reduction (varying resolutions). The expected improvement comes from:\n1. Breakthrough paper analysis of optimal correlation properties in sparse signal processing\n2. Breakthrough paper-derived 25-descriptor optimization landscape suitable with structured feature selection\n3. Dimension-specific to this problem: Sparse structures found in optimal PSK sequences at three distinct resolutions\n\nMathematical Specification\nLet x \u2208 \u211d^N be discretization points with dx=2/N. We optimize:\nh(x) = \u220f_{k=1}^m filter(x - t_k) with t_k \u2208 T (shift set)\n\nConstraints:\n1. \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x) dx = 1 \u2192 (2/N) \u2211 h\u1d62 = 1\n2. 0 \u2264 h\u1d62 \u2264 1 (projected)\n\nObjective:\nMinimize C\u2085 = (2/N) \u00b7 max\u2096 [ \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx ]\n\nWe model via non-negative least-squares with:\n\u2022 Overcomplete filter expansion in space-frequency domain\n\u2022 Group sparsity of shift-invariant components\n\u2022 Dynamic resolution switching between 400, 600, 800 points\n\nImplementation Procedure\n1. Initial Setup:\n   - Import required libraries (scipy, numpy, cvxpy if needed)\n   - Define helper functions for correlation/contact printing\n   - Initialize time budget monitor\n\n2. Multi-resolution Optimization:\n   resolutions = [800, 600, 400]\n   for N in [800,600,400]:\n        if N > ? (previous best), then progress\n\n3. Dynamic Programming with Structured Sparsity:\n   - Initialize via cosine expansion: h\u2080 = \u03a3 \u03b1\u1d62 \u22c5 cos(2\u03c0 i x/2) (projected)\n   - Create sparse filter dictionary of size 3N (overcomplete)\n   - Project onto 1-hot patterns via projected gradient\n\n4. Anderson Accelerated Projected Gradient:\n   - Compute gradient: \u2207C\u2085 = max\u2096 correlation with 1-h interference patterns\n   - Apply: h_{t+1} = proj(nonnegative)(h_t - \u03b1 \u2207C\u2085)\n   - Use Anderson mixing matrix for acceleration\n\n5. Dynamic Resolution:\n   if not converged & time permits & N not last two:\n        new_res = aggressive resolution change (N\u00d71.38 or N/1.38)\n\n6. Comparison Framework:\n   - Track best C\u2085 across resolutions and iterations\n   - Save best at each resolution level with full validation\n\n7. Termination:\n   - Stop when time budget exceeded\n   - Return best among:\n        - Last resolution training\n        - Best from previous resolutions\n        - Upscaled structures\n\nSearch Choices & Usefulness\n1. Resolution variants: [500, 800, 1000] (expect 5-15% improvements)\n2. Sparsity regularization: \u03b2 \u2208 [0.2, 0.8] to balance term\n3. Acceleration factor: \u03b3 \u2208 [0.95, 0.98] for smoothness\n4. Dynamic checking: Switch every 10^6 time units\n\nComputational Budget\nEstimate for N=800 with sparse matrices:\n- Gradient computation: O(N log N)\n- Projection: O(N log N)\n- Budget allocation (80% descents, 20% switches)\n\nFallback Mechanism\n\u2022 Multi-resolution fallback: If any resolution dies, use best from previous\n\u2022 Progressive error checking: Verify all constraints upon final selection",
